\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Which identities need a recovery site}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Each row below is either a theorem already proved, or an
identity whose geometric reading is confined to a
[recovery site](fdip-00PQ). None of these rows extends
a geometric reading past that locus.}

\p{Stokes for a cell subcomplex is \ref{fdip-00O3}, and cellular
Stokes in every degree is \ref{fdip-00OK}. Neither uses a
quadratic form. Square zero for the maximal prolongation is
\ref{fdip-00NW}, and square zero for the cellular coboundary
is the identity used in \ref{fdip-00OX}. A module map induces
the exterior map of \ref{fdip-00QD} over any ring. The
determinant of that map is left open. The geometric product
needs a choice of bilinear form, as in \ref{fdip-00QA}.
Contraction is defined in \ref{fdip-0092} with no form. A
duality identification needs a generator of the top exterior
power, as \ref{fdip-00O5} states, and it fails when that power
is not free of rank one.}

\p{\ref{fdip-00OP} gives #{\extD^2=-\covsq} for positive weights,
and \ref{fdip-00P1} records the same identity for the counting
weights. Reading that square as the Laplacian of the derived
form uses \ref{fdip-00PB}. The commutator square on affine
functions is \ref{fdip-00PI}, and the Clifford relation it
states uses a recovery site. The finite formula of
\ref{fdip-00P2} pairs a cochain with a source term, a boundary
term and a harmonic term. Its reading as a Cauchy formula uses
a recovery site. The harmonic decomposition of \ref{fdip-0090}
uses a positive-definite inner product. The underlying
cochain complex does not use that product.}

\p{The Clifford group in \ref{fdip-00O5} is defined over any
ring, for a supplied form. A versor there is a vector whose
square is a unit. Rotors are not given a separate
identification off a recovery site. Affine links form the
group in \ref{fdip-00OB}. The doubling of \ref{fdip-00PO} and
the degree sign of \ref{fdip-00PT} are stated on recovery
sites, and the volume factor in \ref{fdip-00PT} is the
chirality reading. The signature and the even-subalgebra
isomorphism are \ref{fdip-00Q1} and \ref{fdip-00Q2}. They use
the twisted form where it is defined. They do not extend the
geometric reading off a recovery site.}
